Atlas overlay patch 0063: security/keyring-required
Why: with --password-store=gnome-libsecret (the launcher's explicit store) the engine still falls back,
silently, to a provider with a fixed, public key (tag v10) when the Secret Service is missing, so
cookies and saved passwords would be written under a key anyone can compute. The fixed-key provider
now only reads old v10 data when the explicit keyring store is chosen; it no longer encrypts. Without a
keyring nothing is written (cookies stay session-only, no password is stored) instead of a weak
write. A lower tier (flag, policy, pref) does not exist: the provider list is built in code. The launcher
tells the person (ATLAS-PKG preflight).
Files: components/os_crypt/async/browser/posix_key_provider.h, components/os_crypt/async/browser/posix_key_provider.cc, chrome/browser/browser_process_impl.cc
Test filter: PosixKeyProvider*:OSCryptAsync*; journey atlas-keyring (keyring stopped: no v10 written)
Rebase risk: L (one constructor argument; the list in browser_process_impl.cc changes rarely)
Release: 0.13
License: GPL-3.0-or-later (hunk in browser_process_impl.cc, a file the inherited series patches); BSD-3-Clause for the other two engine files
Verified at: 156.0.8078.79, prepared tree with the inherited and platform series applied

